//! THE COMPILER'S OWN `@input` ACCESSORS (COMPILER DB townhall §7.2,
//! N7): `file_text`/`env` are declared queries over `@std/relation`'s
//! Db, so a relation reading the world outside it — a file's bytes, an
//! environment variable — joins the SAME witness door a row's own
//! fields do. An edit invalidates through the input's own content
//! hash; an unchanged re-read costs no invalidation at all.
//!
//! RECORDED TRIGGER: `compiler/db.av`'s `FileWitness`/
//! `current_file_digest` answer the same question by hand, over the
//! COMPILER's own Kernel-backed `Db` — folding the two into one door
//! needs `@relation`/`@query` wired to THAT `Db`, which §7.5 schedules
//! at M3 ("N6 and N7 land with M3, where the witness first exists"),
//! not yet built. Until then this file is the `@std/relation`-backed
//! half, exercised by `compiler/findings.av`, the one compiler
//! relation already living on that Db.
use @std.relation.{input}
use @std.relation.db.{Db as RelDb}
use @std.io.{read_text, env as os_env}

/// A file's current bytes — never the parse's own copy.
@input
export fn file_text(_db: RelDb, path: string) -> string { read_text(path) catch "" }

/// An environment variable's current value, absent when unset — the
/// same three-way answer `@std/io`'s own `env` gives, never collapsed
/// into "" (CLAUDE.md: an encoding spends the empty value; absence and
/// empty are different answers). Named `env_value`, not `env`: this
/// module already imports `@std.io.env` in several files, and a
/// module's files share one namespace.
@input
export fn env_value(_db: RelDb, key: string) -> string? { os_env(key) }
